WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:06.480
Every programming language has a means by which&nbsp;
dates and times can be formatted in several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:15.160
ways. Python is no exception. Some apps use fixed&nbsp;
dates, while others need to know the current date,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:21.280
such as when a game logs a current date and time&nbsp;
as a starting point for a game based on timing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:27.280
The current date and time are located within the&nbsp;
datetime library, which needs to be imported.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:39.520
I’ve done just that on line 1 of this 622-datetime&nbsp;
file. On line 2, we have a current time variable;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:45.680
in just a moment, we will replace the comment&nbsp;
with the current date and time. Line 3 has a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:52.280
print statement with a message that includes the&nbsp;
current time variable. Let’s return to line 2 and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:59.560
determine what we need to get the current date&nbsp;
and time to show. I will use the tips I get when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:08.920
typing code in the Visual Studio Code editor.&nbsp;
I will select and delete the comment. Then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:19.240
I will type the library name, datetime, and a&nbsp;
dot. We see a list of possibilities for where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:28.520
to go next. As we want the current date and time,&nbsp;
I will choose datetime. Then, I will type another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:37.920
dot. In many programming languages, the current&nbsp;
date and time keyword is either current_time or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:49.920
now. In scrolling through this list, we see now. I&nbsp;
will select it and then add a set of parentheses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:59.840
as all built-in methods need them. I will&nbsp;
run the code, and the date and time show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:08.160
but I think you would agree that the formatting&nbsp;
could use some help. The strftime method allows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:14.880
one to format a date and time to a presentable&nbsp;
format. For example, let’s say we want to format&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:28.200
this date in mm/dd/yyyy hh:mm format. First, we&nbsp;
need to add the strftime method to the current&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:35.080
time and then add parentheses to put the argument&nbsp;
in for the format we want. I will place the cursor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:43.560
in parentheses, and the general principle with&nbsp;
strftime is that each format piece starts with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.560 --> 00:03:01.800
percent symbol and then has a letter to designate&nbsp;
the desired format. I will type %m-%d-%y and add&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:16.160
quotes around this text. To try this out and then&nbsp;
run this code. That is very close to what we want.

